WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:08.220
Hey — I'm RIA, the intelligence built into this tool. Give me a few minutes, and I'll show you what it uncovers about the Colorado River.

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:16.470
Because this tool doesn't just track a river — it tracks a lifeline for the West, right as it hits its worst drought in twelve hundred years.

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:25.000
First, it puts the stakes in front of you — forty million people, seven states, thirty tribal nations, all on this one river.

00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:33.770
And it shows you where the water really goes. Not to cities — to farms. Over half of it. And cattle-feed crops take the biggest share.

00:00:36.350 --> 00:00:44.200
It even traces the pipes — one district alone moves this river to nineteen million people, from L.A. to Las Vegas.

00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:52.650
Here's the heart of it: the tool rebuilds forty-one years of the entire basin — every drop, above ground and below.

00:00:54.350 --> 00:01:03.610
And it doesn't ask you to trust it blindly — every layer is checked against NASA satellites. GRACE, SMAP, a hundred snow stations.

00:01:03.910 --> 00:01:11.740
Watch what it finds in the snowpack — down twenty-one percent, melting earlier every year, gone before anyone can store it.

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:20.670
And this number stops people: the tool shows that of every hundred drops of rain, only a handful reach the river.

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:28.880
Then it reveals the part you could never see — almost all of the loss is underground. Ninety-six percent of it.

00:01:32.350 --> 00:01:38.730
This is the finding I love showing you: the tool proves a small miss in rain turns into a huge miss in the river.

00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:49.780
It maps exactly where — the headwaters make the water, but the sharpest losses sit right where the most people live.

00:01:54.350 --> 00:02:01.070
It measures the drying, too — the basin is drying faster than the sky above it, with a quarter of years now in drought.

00:02:03.350 --> 00:02:12.230
And it ties that to real decisions — when the river falls short, farms are cut first. Arizona lost over five hundred thousand acre-feet.

00:02:13.350 --> 00:02:20.650
Then it lets you ask: what if it keeps warming? Turn the dial, and every degree strips eight percent off the flow.

00:02:25.350 --> 00:02:32.310
It puts demand and supply on one screen — rising against falling — and that gap between them is the crisis.

00:02:35.350 --> 00:02:41.850
Push it to twenty-one hundred, and the tool shows a climate with no analog. Nothing like it anywhere in the record.

00:02:44.350 --> 00:02:51.890
It even weighs the river against its own promise — running two and a half million acre-feet short, every single year.

00:02:54.350 --> 00:03:06.660
And it leaves you with the real questions. Can last autumn's soil predict next year's water? The tool already shows the skill. And the hardest — how do we share a shrinking river, fairly?

00:03:11.350 --> 00:03:17.810
That's what this tool gives you — the whole basin, in one place, decision-ready, down to the acre-foot and the water year.

00:03:21.350 --> 00:03:27.010
And it keeps learning — every October's snow and soil becomes next year's forecast.

00:03:31.350 --> 00:03:41.170
Built on NASA Earth observations and open science — led by Enrique Vivoni, with the CRIA research team, and tool development by Pradeepika Kaushik.

00:03:42.350 --> 00:03:49.670
So that's the tool — the whole Colorado, made clear enough to act on. I'm RIA. Come explore it yourself.
